{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target fails to identify the single mechanism that best explains cross-city variance in the nocturnal UHI gap. It attributes the variance primarily to 'the total volume of masonry and asphalt relative to city size,' but the most well-supported single mechanism in urban climatology is the urban sky view factor (SVF) — the obstruction of the view to the sky by buildings, which traps longwave radiation and suppresses radiative cooling. The target mentions thermal mass and impervious area as the main modulators, which are secondary; by centering the cross-city explanation on stored energy volume rather than radiative geometry, it does not answer the second half of the problem correctly.","counterexample":["Two cities with identical impervious surface area and masonry volume but different building heights and street geometry will have markedly different nocturnal UHI intensities: the denser, taller city has a lower sky view factor, which traps outgoing longwave radiation and slows nighttime cooling far more than the city with wider streets, even though their thermal mass per area is the same."],"premise":"The problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the night-time UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b90864a75220e09b302500bc","quote":"and what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others"}],"target":"4a4ef25c398118f2f27c6d93f5f7d9da4bf024a6caac5c2d4e8b02a3db951d4f"}]}